s($,"bRi","bf0",()=>{var q="hiragana_challenge",p="WELCOME TO",o="JAPANESE",n=null,m="Introduce Yourself",l="INTRODUCE",k="YOURSELF",j="Time to put it together - introduce yourself using what you just learned!",i="GREETINGS &",h="CONVERSATION",g="ME, MY NAME",f="IS...",e="INTRODUCING",d="KATAKANA",c="SMALL",b="\u3084\u30fb\u3086\u30fb\u3088",a="\u3063\u30fb\u30c3",a0="THE JAPANESE",a1="WRITING SYSTEM",a2="kanji_challenge",a3="KANJI STRUCTURE",a4="AND STROKE ORDER",a5="LEARNING VOCABULARY",a6="EFFECTIVELY",a7=t.xu
return A.a([A.md(n,1,n,"New to Japanese? Start here - a friendly overview of the language and its writing system, before we learn your first hiragana.",A.a([A.c9(new A.bbU(),new A.bbV(),1,"Japanese is spoken natively by around 125 million people, almost entirely in Japan - which also makes it one of the most self-contained major languages in the world, since nearly everyone who speaks it lives in one place with one shared culture behind it.",o,p,B.Yq,"Roughly how many people do you think speak Japanese as their native language?"),A.c9(new A.bbW(),new A.bci(),0,"Hiragana is the foundation everything else is built on - every single Japanese sound can be written in it, and it's how children in Japan learn to read and write first. Katakana (for foreign words) and kanji (for core meanings) both come later in your journey - hiragana is where every learner starts.",o,p,B.Xa,"Japanese text actually mixes three different writing systems together. Which one do you think this course starts with?"),A.c9(new A.bct(),new A.bcE(),1,"Hiragana is a syllabary, not an alphabet - each character is a whole syllable rather than a single sound. There are 46 basic characters in total, and once you know them all, you can read any hiragana word out loud, even ones you don't yet know the meaning of.",o,p,B.WB,"Unlike English letters, what does each hiragana character represent?"),A.c9(n,new A.bcP(),n,"Let's meet your first hiragana: \u3042, \u3044, \u3046, \u3048, \u304a - the five vowel sounds - and \u304b, \u304d, \u304f, \u3051, \u3053, the five k-sounds built from them.",n,n,n,n)],a7),"Welcome to Japanese",q),A.md(new A.bd_(),2,1,"A fun, low-pressure creative activity to cap off Day 1 - no quiz this time, just a chance to play with what you already know before \u3055, \u3057, \u3059, \u305b, \u305d and more come next.",B.Yz,"Build Your First Sentences",q),A.md(new A.bda(),7,6,"Your first real conversation phrases - how to greet someone new and say a little about yourself. These flashcards start right at the top of today's session.",A.a([A.c9(new A.bdl(),new A.bdw(),0,"\u306f\u3058\u3081\u307e\u3057\u3066 (hajimemashite) is only ever used the very first time you meet someone - never again after that, even the next time you see the same person.",k,l,B.VA,"When do you think you'd say this phrase?"),A.c9(new A.bbX(),new A.bc7(),0,"\u306f\u3058\u3081\u307e\u3057\u3066 and \u3088\u308d\u3057\u304f usually go together as a pair - \u306f\u3058\u3081\u307e\u3057\u3066 first, then a bit about yourself, then \u3088\u308d\u3057\u304f to close it off.",k,l,B.Yp,"\u3088\u308d\u3057\u304f is short for \u3088\u308d\u3057\u304f\u304a\u9858\u3044\u3057\u307e\u3059. What do you think it roughly means here?"),A.c9(new A.bca(),new A.bcb(),0,'\u3057\u3085\u307f (shumi) means hobby. Slot any word you know into \u3057\u3085\u307f\u306f\uff3f\u3067\u3059 and you\'ve got a real sentence about yourself - \u3067\u3059 just means "is".',k,l,B.Xb,"What do you think \u3057\u3085\u307f means?"),A.c9(n,new A.bcc(),n,j,n,n,n,n)],a7),m,q),A.md(new A.bcd(),8,7,"Everyday greetings for any time of day, plus how to ask someone how they're doing. These flashcards start right at the top of today's session.",A.a([A.c9(new A.bce(),new A.bcf(),0,"Japanese greetings change with the time of day - \u304a\u306f\u3088\u3046\u3054\u3056\u3044\u307e\u3059 for morning, \u3053\u3093\u306b\u3061\u306f for daytime, and \u3053\u3093\u3070\u3093\u306f once evening comes around.",h,i,B.Ze,"Which of these three greetings do you think is used in the morning?"),A.c9(new A.bcg(),new A.bch(),0,'\u3067\u3059 (desu) is one of the most common words in Japanese - it links a word to the sentence, roughly like "is/am/are" in English.',h,i,B.VV,"What job does \u3067\u3059 do in a sentence?"),A.c9(new A.bcj(),new A.bck(),0,"\u304b (ka) at the end of a sentence turns it into a question - \u3067\u3059 becomes \u3067\u3059\u304b, no word order changes needed, unlike English. \u304a is just a polite prefix here.",h,i,B.Xy,"\u304b is added to the very end of a sentence. What do you think it does?"),A.c9(new A.bcl(),new A.bcm(),0,'\u5927\u4e08\u592b (daijoubu) works just like \u5143\u6c17 did - add \u3067\u3059\u304b to ask "are you okay?", or \u3067\u3059 to answer "I\'m okay".',h,i,B.X8,"\u3060\u3044\u3058\u3087\u3046\u3076\u3067\u3059\u304b is another way to check in on someone. What does \u5927\u4e08\u592b mean?"),A.c9(n,new A.bcn(),n,"Let's practice a real greeting exchange with what you just learned.",n,n,n,n)],a7),"Greetings & Basic Conversation",q),A.md(new A.bco(),11,8,"Talking about yourself in more detail - who you are and what belongs to you. These flashcards start right at the top of today's session.",A.a([A.c9(new A.bcp(),new A.bcq(),0,'\u79c1 (watashi) is neutral and safe for anyone to use; \u50d5 (boku) is a more casual "I" typically used by males.',f,g,B.YX,'\u79c1 (watashi) and \u50d5 (boku) both mean "I / me". What\'s the difference?'),A.c9(new A.bcr(),new A.bcs(),0,'\u540d\u524d\u306f\u4f55\u3067\u3059\u304b (namae wa nan desu ka) literally reads "as for [your] name, what is it?" - \u3067\u3059 recap: \u3067\u3059 still just means "is", and \u304b still turns it into a question.',f,g,B.Y7,"\u540d\u524d\u306f\u4f55\u3067\u3059\u304b literally asks about your name. What does \u4f55 mean on its own?"),A.c9(new A.bcu(),new A.bcv(),0,'\u306e (no) is a possessive particle - stick it after \u79c1 to get \u79c1\u306e, "my", then attach any noun you like: \u79c1\u306e\u8da3\u5473 (my hobby), \u79c1\u306e\u540d\u524d (my name), and so on.',f,g,B.YO,"\u306e attaches to \u79c1 to make \u79c1\u306e. What do you think it means?"),A.c9(n,new A.bcw(),n,"One more round of self-introduction practice, this time with \u308f\u305f\u3057\u306e (\u79c1\u306e).",n,n,n,n)],a7),"Me, My Name Is...",q),A.md(n,9,n,"A second script for a second job - what katakana is for, and why it's easier than it looks. These flashcards start right at the top of today's session.",A.a([A.c9(new A.bcx(),new A.bcy(),0,"Katakana covers the same sounds as hiragana, just in different-looking characters - it's mainly reserved for words borrowed from other languages, foreign names, and the occasional bit of emphasis (a bit like italics in English).",d,e,B.Zt,"What do you think katakana is mainly used for?"),A.c9(new A.bcz(),new A.bcA(),0,"The moment you see katakana, you can usually guess a word is borrowed from another language - often you can sound it out and hear the original English word hiding inside.",d,e,B.Yr,"Which languages do you think \u30d4\u30b6 (piza) and \u30b3\u30fc\u30d2\u30fc (koohii) were borrowed from?"),A.c9(new A.bcB(),new A.bcC(),0,"This is the good news about katakana: you already know every sound in it from hiragana. You're just learning new shapes to write sounds you can already say - each new character will show you its hiragana match for comparison as you go.",d,e,B.XL,'\u30a2 and \u3042 are both read "a". Are you learning a brand new sound here?'),A.c9(n,new A.bcD(),n,"Let's meet your first katakana: \u30a2, \u30a4, \u30a6, \u30a8, \u30aa - the five vowel sounds - and \u30ab, \u30ad, \u30af, \u30b1, \u30b3, the five k-sounds built from them. Sound familiar? That's the idea!",n,n,n,n)],a7),"Introducing Katakana",q),A.md(n,17,n,"A quick one today - no new flashcards, just a look at how kana combine to make new sounds. Take your time with this one; it'll click once real words start using it.",A.a([A.c9(new A.bcF(),new A.bcG(),0,"Written small and tucked right after certain kana (\u304d, \u3057, \u3061, \u306b, \u3072, \u307f, \u308a and their dakuten versions), \u3083/\u3085/\u3087 fuse the two into a single blended syllable - these combinations are called youon.",b,c,B.Ye,"These same three kana also come in a small size: \u3083 \u3085 \u3087. What do you think that small size means?"),A.c9(new A.bcH(),new A.bcI(),0,"Combined kana like this are always pronounced as one single beat, never as two separate sounds - \u304d and \u3083 blend into exactly one syllable.",b,c,B.WO,"How many syllables/beats is \u304d\u3083 (kya) pronounced as?"),A.c9(n,new A.bcJ(),n,"Here's the full set for reference - you don't need to memorize this chart right now, just know it exists. You'll naturally pick these up as real words introduce them.",b,c,n,n),A.c9(new A.bcK(),new A.bcL(),0,"Katakana has the exact same small \u30e3/\u30e5/\u30e7 combinations, and they show up constantly in loanwords - \u30ad\u30e3, \u30b7\u30e3, \u30c1\u30e7, and so on.",b,c,B.Zh,"\u30ad\u30e3\u30f3\u30d7 (kyanpu) uses a small \u30e3. What do you think it borrows from English?"),A.c9(n,new A.bcM(),n,"No flashcards today - just let this sink in. You'll meet these combinations naturally as you keep going.",n,n,n,n)],a7),"Small \u3084, \u3086, \u3088",q),A.md(n,18,n,"Two more quick building blocks - no new flashcards today either, just two symbols you'll see everywhere once real words start appearing.",A.a([A.c9(new A.bcN(),new A.bcO(),0,"A small \u3063 (sokuon) creates a tiny pause before the next consonant, effectively doubling it - it's never pronounced on its own.",a,c,B.Xp,"What do you think the small \u3063 in \u304c\u3063\u3053\u3046 (school) does?"),A.c9(new A.bcQ(),new A.bcR(),0,"Small \u3063/\u30c3 look identical in shape to the full-size \u3064/\u30c4, just noticeably smaller and shifted slightly - that's the only visual difference.",a,c,B.XM,"Katakana has a small \u30c3 too, working exactly the same way. What sport do you think \u30b5\u30c3\u30ab\u30fc (sakkaa) is?"),A.c9(new A.bcS(),new A.bcT(),0,"\u30fc (ch\u014donpu) simply extends the vowel sound right before it - it only appears in katakana; hiragana stretches vowels by writing the actual vowel again instead (e.g. \u304a\u304b\u3042\u3055\u3093).",a,c,B.VE,"What do you think the \u30fc mark does in katakana words like \u30b3\u30fc\u30d2\u30fc?"),A.c9(n,new A.bcU(),n,"Easy to mix up at a glance, but each is distinct: \u30fc is a plain dash that only ever appears inside a katakana word; 1 is a number, never mixed in with kana; \u3063 (or katakana \u30c3) is a small, tsu-shaped character, not a straight line at all.",a,c,n,n),A.c9(n,new A.bcV(),n,"No flashcards today either - both of these will show up constantly once we get to real words, so just get familiar with the idea. Tomorrow you'll drill a few real words that use small \u3063.",n,n,n,n)],a7),"Small \u3063 and \u30fc",q),A.md(n,1,n,"A quick, friendly primer on how Japanese writing works, before we meet today's kanji.",A.a([A.c9(new A.bcW(),new A.bcX(),1,"Japanese text mixes all three together in the same sentence - you're about to see how.",a1,a0,B.WE,"Which of these do you think is used for foreign words like pizza or coffee?"),A.c9(new A.bcY(),new A.bcZ(),1,"The kanji carries the core meaning (eat); the hiragana ending changes with tense and politeness - \u98df\u3079\u305f (ate), \u98df\u3079\u307e\u3059 (eat, polite), and so on.",a1,a0,B.X7,"Which part do you think changes depending on tense or politeness?"),A.c9(new A.bd0(),new A.bd1(),1,"It's called furigana, and it simply shows how to pronounce the kanji next to it - handy for rare readings, or for learners.",a1,a0,B.Y0,"What do you think small hiragana written above a kanji is for?"),A.c9(new A.bd2(),new A.bd3(),0,"Loanwords from other languages are written in katakana - even ones that don't look foreign at first, like \u30a2\u30d9\u30c3\u30af (from French 'avec') or \u30a2\u30eb\u30d0\u30a4\u30c8 (from German 'Arbeit').",a1,a0,B.Yh,"Which of these do you think is originally a Japanese word?"),A.c9(new A.bd4(),new A.bd5(),2,"Foreign names too. Katakana can also add emphasis to a native word, similar to italics or ALL CAPS in English.",a1,a0,B.Xj,"Do you think foreign names are written in kanji, hiragana, or katakana?"),A.c9(new A.bd6(),new A.bd7(),2,"\u6d88 (kanji) means 'erase', \u3057 is a hiragana grammar link, and \u30b4\u30e0 (katakana) comes from the Dutch word for rubber, 'gom'. Three systems, one everyday word: eraser.",a1,a0,B.ZF,"Tap the part you think is katakana."),A.c9(new A.bd8(),new A.bd9(),0,"Kanji breaks a sentence into meaningful chunks, a bit like the spaces between English words - the all-hiragana version is technically correct, just much harder to scan.",a1,a0,B.Zu,"Which sentence do you think is easier to scan at a glance?"),A.c9(new A.bdb(),new A.bdc(),1,"Kanji were originally taken from China. After WWII, Japan simplified thousands of complex kanji to make them faster to write - the old forms (\u65e7\u5b57\u4f53) now mostly show up in names and old texts.",a1,a0,B.Z4,"Which do you think is the modern, simplified form?"),A.c9(n,new A.bdd(),n,"Now let's meet today's kanji: \u65e5, \u738b, \u7389, \u53e3, \u4eba, \u5165, \u706b and more!",n,n,n,n)],a7),"Kanji Essentials",a2),A.md(new A.bde(),91,1,"A quick break from kanji to put \u8a00 (the one you just met today) to use - how to introduce yourself in Japanese.",A.a([A.c9(new A.bdf(),new A.bdg(),0,"Either works to open a self-introduction - \u306f\u3058\u3081\u307e\u3057\u3066 if it's genuinely the first time you're meeting someone, \u3053\u3093\u306b\u3061\u306f as a general hello any time.",k,l,B.VN,"\u306f\u3058\u3081\u307e\u3057\u3066 and \u3053\u3093\u306b\u3061\u306f can both open an introduction. When specifically is \u306f\u3058\u3081\u307e\u3057\u3066 used?"),A.c9(new A.bdh(),new A.bdi(),0,'\u8a00\u3046 (iu) literally means "to say". Attach it right after a name and you get "[name] \u3068\u3044\u3046", literally "is said as [name]" - a common way to introduce yourself.',k,l,B.YP,"You just met \u8a00 (say) today. What do you think \u8a00\u3046 (iu) means as a whole word?"),A.c9(new A.bdj(),new A.bdk(),0,'Slot your name in before either one: \u30ed\u30a4\u30c9\u3068\u3044\u3044\u307e\u3059 ("I\'m called Lloyd") is a safe, polite way to introduce yourself. Foreign names are usually written in katakana, even inside a Japanese sentence.',k,l,B.XK,"\u3068\u3044\u3044\u307e\u3059 is the polite version of \u3068\u3044\u3046. Which is more appropriate when meeting someone new?"),A.c9(n,new A.bdm(),n,j,n,n,n,n)],a7),m,a2),A.md(n,2,n,"How kanji are built, and how to write them - a few ideas that'll help for the rest of the challenge.",A.a([A.c9(new A.bdn(),new A.bdo(),0,"These are pictographs - kanji that started as simple drawings of the thing they represent, then got simplified and squared off over thousands of years into the shapes we use today.",a4,a3,B.WD,"How do you think kanji like these first came about?"),A.c9(new A.bdp(),new A.bdq(),1,"\u706b (fire) shows up inside all three, sometimes reshaped into \u706c (four little dots) at the bottom. Spotting a radical like this instantly tells you the kanji is probably fire or heat related - that's the first big reason radicals are useful.",a4,a3,B.XO,"These all share a hidden piece related to one basic kanji. Which one?"),A.c9(new A.bdr(),new A.bds(),0,"All three are read with \u304e (gi), inherited from the shared \u7fa9 component. This is the second reason radicals are useful - they can hint at the reading, not just the meaning.",a4,a3,B.YT,"\u5100, \u72a0, and \u8b70 all share a component (\u7fa9) that hints at their reading. Which reading do you think they share?"),A.c9(new A.bdt(),new A.bdu(),0,"\u7389 has a tiny extra dot that \u738b doesn't. Kanji like these - and \u4eba vs \u5165 - are easy to mix up at a glance, so it pays to look closely at the small details.",a4,a3,B.Xq,"King (\u738b) and ball (\u7389) look almost identical. Can you spot the difference?"),A.c9(new A.bdv(),new A.bdx(),0,'Following the standard stroke order helps your kanji look right, and over time builds the same kind of muscle memory as learning to type - your hand starts to "know" the kanji.',a4,a3,B.XP,"Besides making your kanji look neater, why else do you think stroke order matters?"),A.c9(new A.bdy(),new A.bdz(),1,"This is one of the main exceptions to the usual rules: that wrapping 'walking' radical (\u8fb6/\u5ef4) is drawn last, even though it looks like it should come first.",a4,a3,B.X4,"These both have a 'walking' shape wrapping around the left and bottom. Drawn first or last?"),A.c9(n,new A.bdA(),n,"Now let's meet today's kanji: \u5927, \u529b, \u5200, \u6728, \u6c34, \u7236, \u7530, \u96e8, \u6708, \u5973!",n,n,n,n),A.c9(new A.bdB(),new A.bdC(),n,"Kanji and vocabulary reinforce each other - the Essential Vocabulary Challenge introduces new words at your own pace alongside the kanji you're already learning here. Tap above whenever you're ready to begin (or just carry on with today's kanji for now).",n,n,n,n)],a7),"Kanji Structure & Stroke Order",a2),A.md(n,1,n,"A quick primer on how to actually learn a word - not just recognize it - before we start building your Essential Vocabulary.",A.a([A.c9(n,new A.bdD(),n,"Some of these words use kanji you haven't learned yet - don't worry about that for now. They'll come naturally as you work through the 90 Day Kanji Challenge and get more immersed in the language. For today, just focus on the words themselves.",a6,a5,n,n),A.c9(new A.bdE(),new A.bdF(),1,"\u5fc5\u8981 (\u3072\u3064\u3088\u3046) means 'necessary'. A word has three things worth learning: what it means, how it's written (kanji/kana), and how it's pronounced.",a6,a5,B.Xf,"Which part of this word tells you HOW to say it out loud?"),A.c9(new A.bdG(),new A.bbY(),1,"Truly knowing a word means being solid on all four - not just being able to recognize its meaning when you see it.",a6,a5,B.VI,"Besides meaning, written form, and pronunciation, what's the fourth thing worth learning about a word?"),A.c9(new A.bbZ(),new A.bc_(),0,"An invented memory aid: imagine yourself with a water mustache (\u308f\u305f- sounds like 'water'), or picture yourself as a tree (\u79be) thinking about something personal (\u30e0) - private thoughts. A vivid, even silly image sticks far better than the word alone.",a6,a5,B.XG,"Can you guess how \u79c1 (I/me) is read?"),A.c9(new A.bc0(),new A.bc1(),0,"This is context in action - picking the right word for the social situation, not just knowing a dictionary meaning.",a6,a5,B.VT,'\u79c1, \u50d5, and \u4ffa all mean "I/me" - so what actually differs between them?'),A.c9(new A.bc2(),new A.bc3(),0,"Seeing a literal breakdown next to the natural translation helps you understand what each particle is doing - Tangorin is a great free resource for finding real example sentences like this.",a6,a5,B.Yc,"A literal, word-for-word translation often sounds awkward in English. Is that a problem?"),A.c9(new A.bc4(),new A.bc5(),0,"Typing (or tapping a flashcard) is fast for daily review, but writing by hand builds a deeper, more physical kind of memory - that's what this app's Practice Writing and stroke-order tools are for.",a6,a5,B.Z7,"Do you think writing a word out by hand teaches you something typing doesn't?"),A.c9(new A.bc6(),new A.bc8(),1,"Meeting a word again naturally - in a story, a song, a sentence you're reading or listening to - reinforces it far more than drilling it in isolation. That's exactly what this app's Reading and Listening tabs are for.",a6,a5,B.Zm,"Which do you think helps a word stick best in the long run?"),A.c9(n,new A.bc9(),n,"Time to start building your Essential Vocabulary - keep all four aspects in mind as you go!",n,n,n,n)],a7),"Learning Vocabulary Effectively","essential_vocab")],A.aE("F<Xl>"))})
